  "body": "According to the current spec, when a dictionary is provided for `setTransform()`, do the following:\r\n\r\n> The setTransform(transform) method, when invoked, must reset the current transformation matrix to the value represented by the result of creating a DOMMatrix from a dictionary transform.\r\n\r\nThis does not have a clear explanation of what to do in the case that `{a: NaN}` or `{a: {}}` is given.  The existing `setTransform()` specifies how to handle this situation:\r\n\r\n> If any of the arguments are infinite or NaN, then abort these steps.\r\n\r\nIf we follow this, then `{a: NaN}` would have no effect, as it would bail before doing anything.  I think that this is the desired behavior, but it would nice to have clarification.  I doubt that developers would want parts of a DOMMatrix to be applied while others are ignored.  Additionally, this would match the existing functionality of `setTransform()`.\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// This is not desired, as parts of the transform applied while others were ignored.\r\nctx.setTransform(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7);\r\nctx.setTransform({a: NaN, b: 0});\r\nctx.getTransform(); // [1, 0, 4, 5, 6, 7] => BAD!\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n// This matches the current functionality of setTransform(), as it's clear that nothing changed due to the NaN.\r\nctx.setTransform(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7);\r\nctx.setTransform({a: NaN, b: 0});\r\nctx.getTransform(); // [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7] => GOOD!\r\n```\r\n\r\nThanks! 😃 \r\n\r\nAdding @grorg since he proposed `setTransform`.",
